From 5454c4a9d2f222e51e9b33aee32ad7eb031ee7c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yin Li <kevinli.ai.work@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 11:51:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [Perf][Beam Search] Don't materialize dense grammar
 allowed-token sets

With structured outputs, offline beam search fills a grammar bitmask per beam
per step, turns it into a list of every allowed token ID
(_bitmask_to_token_ids), and _beam_search_step turns that list into a set()
only to test the ~2 * beam_width candidate tokens against it. Inside free-form
strings a JSON grammar allows almost the whole vocabulary, so each beam built
and hashed a ~147k-element list and set per step to answer ~64 lookups.

Unpack the packed row once into a bool array over the vocabulary
(np.unpackbits on its raw bytes on a little-endian CPU host, the previous
gather/shift path otherwise) and answer membership by bit lookup through a
small _AllowedTokens wrapper. The explicit ID list is built only when it is
handed to the engine, i.e. when the grammar allows at most
_MAX_NUM_ALLOWED_TOKEN_IDS tokens, as before. Dropping beams whose grammar is
terminated or allows nothing is unchanged. The packed row is copied before it
is viewed from NumPy so the caller's bitmask never shares storage with it.

Measured with vLLM's XgrammarBackend, the Qwen1.5 tokenizer (vocab 151,936)
and a JSON-schema grammar, 32 beams spread over a 53-token conforming
trajectory (the real mix of structural and in-string positions; 36 of 56
states along it allow > 1024 tokens, median 147,123), CPU only, one pinned
core, `_build_beam_sampling_params` plus the step's candidate lookups:

  main:         206.0 / 216.9 ms per step (two runs)
  this change:    6.9 /   6.3 ms per step

A faster unpack alone would not get there: on these masks it is only
1.7-2.7x, because the dense case is dominated by building the Python list
and set, which this change avoids.

Equivalence on those 56 real grammar states (plus a terminated and an
off-grammar beam): identical drop decisions, identical allowed_token_ids
handed to the engine, and identical membership answers for every token ID,
including IDs past the vocabulary.

Tests cover the bit layout on both unpack paths (sign bits, padding bits and
words past the vocabulary, vocab sizes around word boundaries), non-contiguous
rows, the caller's row staying resizable and independent, membership bounds,
the engine-list cap on both sides of 1024, and dropped beams; a wrong bit
order, a missing bounds check, an off-by-one at the cap or keeping dead-end
beams each fail them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
